WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
API security lasts when it is tested and reviewed as part of ordinary delivery.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:11.800
Unit tests can exercise validation helpers, policy functions, and response mappers.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:19.500
Integration tests can confirm route behavior with real middleware, authentication context, and persistence.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:28.160
Negative tests are especially important because they show that invalid input, missing identity, and unauthorized object access are rejected consistently.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:36.200
Authorization matrix tests help teams avoid gaps between roles, permissions, tenants, and resource ownership.

6
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:44.620
Contract tests and OpenAPI review can reveal missing validation, inconsistent status codes, or response shapes that expose more than intended.

7
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.480
These tests are not a replacement for design review, but they turn important expectations into repeatable checks.

8
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.120
Code review should ask concrete questions.

9
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.700
What does this route trust?

10
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.480
Which caller is expected?

11
00:00:58.580 --> 00:00:59.940
What input is accepted?

12
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.200
Where is runtime validation performed?

13
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.300
Which object is being authorized?

14
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.320
What fields leave the API?

15
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.620
What gets logged?

16
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.640
What happens when the dependency is slow or unavailable?

17
00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.380
Specific questions make review more useful than a generic security reminder.

18
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:26.820
Secure delivery includes dependency review, CI checks, environment configuration, secret handling, release gates, and production telemetry.

19
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.560
Findings should become tracked engineering work.

20
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:40.300
A durable REST API security program is not a final checklist at the end of a sprint; it is a set of repeatable habits that keep routes, contracts, and operations aligned over time.

21
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.420
Delivery governance should make secure behavior visible.

22
00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:52.520
Dashboards, release notes, pull request templates, and runbooks can all remind teams what changed and how it was verified.

23
00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:58.140
The goal is not paperwork; the goal is evidence that important controls still work after the API evolves.
